st move from the Valid Moves list above. You can choose to pass even if you have valid moves that can beat the previous play, but you cannot pass if you are in a Free Play (no last play).\nEnsure your response matches one of the valid moves exactly.\nRemember to provide Explanation, Reason, and Answer.", 'reasoning': "Okay, here's the situation as I see it, and I'm a little stressed. I'm a peasant, stuck between this aggressive Landlord and my teammate, and it's my turn. The Landlord just laid down a straight from 9 to Ace, and I have a bomb, but my hand is a mess otherwise. The Landlord has only 9 cards left! That's it, that's almost nothing! He already played a boat and then this straight. I need to figure out what to do.\n\nFirst, let's analyze the opposition. This Landlord has been playing aggressively, clearing out cards fast. He's got 9 cards left, and I need to try to stop him from going out. I need to remember he played a full house and this straight. He has been taking initiative all round. He might have the big cards, the Jokers, or the twos.\n\nNow let's check my hand. I have a bomb, thank god, but it leaves me with a collection of singles and low pairs. My best move is to bomb and then pass the turn to my teammate! Should I save the bomb? No way. Saving the bomb just lets the Landlord walk all over us. But is this my best shot? I don't see any other options. Passing lets him walk, and if I don't have the 7 for a good straight after bombing, then using it is not as bad. If I save the bomb, the Landlord can just play out the game.\n\nWhat's my plan then? Bomb! That should force him to use his big cards. Then, to make sure my teammate can get involved, I will lay down a low card. It's time to interrupt the Landlord's momentum and try to force him to stop! I have to fight for the peasants. Let's do this!\n\n\n", 'output': 'Evaluation: The Landlord has only 9 cards remaining and has dominated the game so far, playing a Full House and a high Straight (9-A) without interruption. Passing here would grant the Landlord another free turn, which is extremely dangerous given his low card count; he could easily play a large combination to go out or leave himself with one easy move. Although my hand is relatively weak (17 cards, scattered structure) and bombing leaves me with 13 cards, I must act as a gatekeeper to break the Landlord\'s momentum. Using the Bomb (6666) is the only way to stop the current play. Since I lack a 7 to connect my 3,4,5,6,8 cards into a straight, the 6s are most valuable as a bomb. If the bomb clears, I can shift to playing a small single (4) to coordinate with my teammate (who has a full hand of 17 cards) or force the Landlord to use his top cards (2s/Jokers).\n\nReason: The Landlord is threatening to win with only 9 cards left. I must use my Bomb to stop his high straight (9-A) and regain initiative for the Peasant team. Passing is too passive and risky.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♠6", "♥6", "♣6", "♦6"]}'}
