rd to protect my teammate.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♥4"]}\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: My hand consists of five singles: 2, 10, 9, 5, 4. I currently have the lead (Free Play). The Landlord (Next Player) has 7 cards remaining and passed on my previous play of a single Ace. This strongly suggests the Landlord does not have a single 2 (or is unwilling to break a pair of 2s or a bomb/rocket) and likely lacks high singles. My Teammate (Previous Player) has 6 cards. Playing my ♣2 now would guarantee a trick win but leave me with four weak singles (10, 9, 5, 4) and no entry, effectively removing me from the game.\n\nReason: The optimal move is to play my lowest single, ♥4. This serves two purposes:\n1. It forces the Landlord to play. If the Landlord has low "junk" cards, they will be forced to play them, allowing my Teammate (who plays after the Landlord) to beat them with a mid-range card and take the lead.\n2. It preserves my ♣2 as a control card. I can use it later to intercept the Landlord if they try to pass a high card or to regain the lead when my hand is smaller.\nSince the Landlord passed on an Ace, playing a low card puts pressure on them to either break a set or let my teammate in cheaply.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♥4"]}'}
